    "summary": "Boy’s shirt (adire), made up from three pieces of imported European machine white calico cotton, indigo-dyed in Africa with a design drawn by hand in starch resist and then dyed in a single shade of indigo. The cotton was decorated with a repeated pattern of knots and flowers. One piece was cut to form the body with a circular hole cut for the head, and two pieces were cut to form the short sleeves; the whole was machine-sewn together. The neck of the garment has been lined with a piece of European printed cloth with an ikat design in maroon, black and white, and machine stitched with ornamental circles in white cotton; a small area at the bottom of the shirt has been lined with a white and blue European brocaded cotton.",
